sequenceDiagram
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Gateway holds each Route-serving Node from admission to the terminal state; a second Route write on it waits (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run, certificate signing is the one step-to-Gateway call. docs/reference/routes.md#change-the-target-of-a-route.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: route:target:set
  %% kind: route.target_set
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as Route Nodes<br/>workload · Router
  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS
  CLI->>GW: route:target:set<br/>ROUTE TARGET
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit route:target:set ROUTE TARGET`
  %%> - syntax: `<route> <target> [--targets=ID]... [--reassign=ID:ROUTE]... [--remove=ID]... [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Route and its target Instance; the options send the complete target set of a production pool and a disposition for each detached Instance
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Guard, lock, record intent<br/>targets unchanged
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the Route's Nodes
  %%> - the target is an active Instance of the Route's Project; a production pool is a Cluster-scoped explicit Route with active production Instances on distinct active `app-prod` Nodes
  %%> - the change does not take an Instance from another Route, or detach an active Instance from its only Route (`route.target_conflict`); a detached Instance has a disposition (`route.target_disposition_required`)
  %%> - no other target-set change is recorded on the Route (`route.target_set_conflict`) and no other operation holds the projection (`app-dev.projection_busy`)
  %%> Result:
  %%> - a single-target change stores the association before the run; a production pool records the requested set and keeps the associations unchanged until the run's result
  %%> - an `active` Route refuses a single-target change (`route.reconciliation_required`)
  %%> - `--remove` is refused today (`route.target_cleanup_unavailable`, 503)
  alt unpublished development Route
    Note over GW: Record the target<br/>R1: no run
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - changes only Orbit's record; no Node changes and no run starts
    %%> Repeat:
    %%> - setting the current target again is skipped: `Skipped — target already set (route.target_unchanged)`
    GW-->>CLI: Target set<br/>task succeeded
  else published Route
    Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - holds each Node that serves this Route's site (Caddy and PHP) from admission to the terminal state
    %%> - a second Route write on one of those Nodes waits (task status `waiting`) and starts when the Node is free, oldest first
    %%> Not held:
    %%> - the DNS Node: its records are keyed, and the apply phase refuses a file that changed since the read
    %%> Limit:
    %%> - two Route writes on one Node run one after the other, so each waits for the other's whole run
    %%> - a newer write waits behind an older waiting one that shares a Node, and a waiting write can be cancelled
    %%> - a timed-out write keeps its Nodes until Swamp confirms that its run stopped, or for 15 minutes
    %%> - task provision and cancel, instance:register and the Cluster Router writes hold the Nodes for their Route-publish step only, through the `route-hold` call
    %%> - a task that waits starts from the Node's live files; a site block that matches none of its captured states still stops it with `owned_revision_conflict`
    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-route-target-set
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID
    %%> What you get:
    %%> - the task ID, at once
    %%> Next:
    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
    SW->>N: Make CSRs on new Nodes<br/>openssl req · key stays on Node
    %%> Key custody:
    %%> - the CSR action creates the leaf directory and gives its Node-local key root:caddy ownership and mode 0640 before it returns
    SW->>GW: Sign the CSRs<br/>operation:direct:certificate-sign
    %%> Why a call to the Gateway:
    %%> - certificate signing is the one allowed step-to-Gateway call; the Orbit CA stays in the Gateway
    GW-->>SW: Signed certificates<br/>Orbit CA key stays in Gateway
    opt published development Route
      SW->>N: Install cert, publish site<br/>route:create's publish step
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - moves the site to the new checkout; the target stays set if this fails (`route.publish_failed`), and the request is run again
    end
    opt production pool
      SW->>N: Stage the new targets<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · firewall
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - stages each added target's runtime, certificates, Caddy site and `.env`
      SW->>N: Probe each new target<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · SNI domain
      %%> Why:
      %%> - the probe verifies the Route's hostname and the Orbit CA over TLS; application HTTP errors do not fail it, and a failed probe stops the switch
      SW->>N: Switch the Router pool<br/>Router Caddy · reload
      SW->>N: Retire detached targets<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt · absent
    end
    opt Node-scoped, outside TLD
      SW->>V: Move private DNS<br/>records + catalog, then reload
    end
    alt every step succeeds
      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
      Note over GW: Write the new targets<br/>from result
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the new associations are written, and a destination Route that received a reassigned Instance becomes `active`
    else a step fails
      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
      Note over GW: Mark the change failed<br/>targets from failed step
      %%> Result for a published development Route:
      %%> - the new target stays set and the failed step is `Install cert, publish site`; run the same request again
      %%> Result for a production pool:
      %%> - before the Router switch, the original associations stay and the staged sites serve no traffic
      %%> - after the switch, the new associations are written, because the new pool serves, and the recorded set and `failed_step` stay
      %%> On the Nodes:
      %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback
      %%> Retry:
      %%> - run the same request again; a different request is refused until the change completes (`route.target_set_conflict`); a request with dispositions can be refused with `route.target_disposition_required` once the detached Instances are gone
    end
    loop every 2 s until terminal
      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
      GW-->>CLI: status + result
    end
  end
